 For each common scored row report all three Brier losses and two improvements: `(p_B0_exposure - y)^2 - (p_B1 - y)^2` and `(p_B0_pooled - y)^2 - (p_B1 - y)^2`. Positive means B1 has lower squared probability error than the named comparator. Do not substitute one favorable comparison for the other. For the overall descriptive comparison, first average losses over each exposure's available outer-test origins, then average equally over exposures with common support. Report any exposure without support and the actual exposure/date coverage. Do not call the observed subset a complete-universe score when it is not. Also retain each year's results so long histories cannot hide recent failure.
 
-Use only the previously proposed calibration bins `[0,.1)`, `[.1,.2)`, `[.2,.4)`, `[.4,.6)`, `[.6,1]`. Report mean probability, observed positive-origin fraction, raw origin/exposure counts, weight mass and weighted absolute bin error for all three specifications on the same scored population. Empty bins remain empty. No calibrator is fitted. Dependent origins are not Bernoulli-independent trials; no confidence interval, significance, power or precise calibration grade is supplied by this slice. `independent_episode_count` remains null until an appropriate dependence design is separately bound. Four pooled overlap groups do not establish four independent crises.
+**Equal-exposure reporting weights (R1).** Apply the following definitions separately to each existing reporting population `G`: the overall common-scored population, each annual population, each existing exposure/year cell, and the two saved damage strata. The damage strata retain the saved true/false classification from section 3; null is assigned to neither stratum. These definitions add no reporting intersections or model fits. Let `E_G` be the exposures with at least one common-scored row in `G`, and let `n[e,G]` be exposure `e`'s row count there. For each row `i` of exposure `e`, use evaluation weight `q_i = 1 / (|E_G| * n[e,G])`. The identical population and row weights apply to exposure-B0, pooled-B0 and B1; their weights sum to one when `G` is nonempty.
+
+Define `Brier(model,G) = sum_{i in G} q_i * (p_model[i] - y[i])^2`. The paired improvements are `Brier(B0_EXPOSURE,G) - Brier(B1,G)` and `Brier(B0_POOLED,G) - Brier(B1,G)`, equivalently the weighted averages of the corresponding row-level improvements. For an empty population, retain zero support counts and zero weight mass, with unavailable/null Brier, improvement and aggregate calibration metrics; do not report zero error.
+
+Weights are re-equalized within each reporting population. Annual and damage panels therefore need not recombine into the overall result using raw row counts or equal panel weights. Differences between damage strata can reflect their exposure/date composition. The runner must bind the existing panel list and these formulas before estimation; no alternative weighting may be selected after results.
+
+Use only the previously proposed calibration bins `[0,.1)`, `[.1,.2)`, `[.2,.4)`, `[.4,.6)`, `[.6,1]`. For each model and reporting population `G`, bin membership is determined by that model's own prediction: `K[model,k,G] = {i in G: p_model[i] lies in bin k}`. All three models retain the same underlying scored population and `q_i`; their membership within a given bin may differ.
+
+For a nonempty bin, define `M[model,k,G] = sum_{i in K} q_i`, weighted mean probability `pbar = sum_{i in K} q_i * p_model[i] / M`, and weighted positive-origin fraction `ybar = sum_{i in K} q_i * y[i] / M`. Report the absolute bin error `abs(pbar - ybar)` separately from its weighted contribution `M * abs(pbar - ybar)`. Also report raw origin, raw positive-origin and exposure counts separately from weight mass. If an aggregate weighted-bin error is reported for a nonempty population, it is the sum of the model's weighted bin contributions.
+
+An empty bin has zero counts and mass, null mean probability, null positive-origin fraction and null absolute bin error. Its weighted contribution is zero by the zero-mass convention; that value supplies no evidence of calibration. An empty reporting population retains null aggregate calibration metrics as specified above. No calibrator is fitted. Dependent origins are not Bernoulli-independent trials; no confidence interval, significance, power or precise calibration grade is supplied by this slice. `independent_episode_count` remains null until an appropriate dependence design is separately bound. Four pooled overlap groups do not establish four independent crises.
 
